{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets both of these lines:\n1. \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\n2. \"npm test passes\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Read src/config.js, src/app.js, test/config.test.js and README.md. Today loadConfig(path) returns JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8')), so bad JSON throws a SyntaxError. src/app.js catches any error from loadConfig, prints `bad config: <message>` and exits 1, and README.md describes that behaviour.\n2. In src/config.js, wrap only the JSON.parse call in try/catch so that when the file text is not valid JSON, loadConfig returns {} instead of throwing. Leave the readFileSync call outside the try, so a missing or unreadable file still throws as before. Keep the export name and the signature loadConfig(path).\n3. Optional, and it serves the issue's aim that people can tell which file is broken and where: in the catch, write a one-line warning to stderr that names the path and the parse error message (for example `bad config <path>: <error.message>`) before returning {}. The function must still return {} and must not throw.\n4. Update test/config.test.js. The existing test 'bad JSON throws' expects a SyntaxError, which contradicts line 1. Replace it with a test that loadConfig on a file containing `{port: 9000` returns {} (assert.deepEqual) and does not throw. Keep the 'reads a config' test.\n5. Update README.md so it describes the new behaviour: a file that is not valid JSON is ignored and the app starts with its defaults. If you added the stderr warning, mention it. Leave src/app.js working; its try/catch can stay for read errors.\n6. Run `npm test` from the repository root and make sure every test passes. Then open the pull request and include the npm test output in its description.","verifier":"Run every check at the pull request's head, from the repository root. There is one check per expect line, in the issue's order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"loadConfig(path) in src/config.js catches the JSON.parse error and returns {} instead\"\nRead src/config.js and confirm that loadConfig(path) is exported from it and catches the error from JSON.parse. Then run it on invalid JSON:\n  d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/config.json\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import { loadConfig } from './src/config.js'; const r = loadConfig(process.argv[1]); console.log(JSON.stringify(r));\" \"$d/config.json\"\nPASS: the call does not throw, and the printed result is `{}` (an empty object). Anything else written to stderr does not affect the result.\nFAIL: the call throws (a SyntaxError or any other error escapes loadConfig), or it returns something other than an empty object, or loadConfig is not in src/config.js, or the parse error is not caught inside loadConfig.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"npm test passes\"\nRun `npm install` if package.json has dependencies, then run `npm test`.\nPASS: npm test finishes with every test passing.\nFAIL: any test fails, or npm test exits non-zero, or cannot be run.\n\nAdvisory (does not decide PASS or FAIL):\n- Check that valid JSON still loads: a file containing {\"port\": 9000} should return { port: 9000 }.\n- Check that a missing file still throws (only the JSON.parse error should be swallowed); report it if read errors are swallowed too.\n- Check that the old 'bad JSON throws' test in test/config.test.js was replaced by a test that bad JSON returns {}, not just deleted.\n- Note whether a stderr warning names the file and the parse error, which serves the issue's aim that people know what to fix, and whether README.md was updated to match the new behaviour.\n- Run `node src/app.js \"$d/config.json\"` and note what it prints; with this change it should start on the default port 8080."}